\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dynamics of magnetically-confined charged particles exhibits three orbital
 time scales associated with gyromotion, bounce motion, and drift motion.
 To lowest order, the fast gyromotion involves circular motion of a charged
 particle (with mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 and charge 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

) in the plane transverse to a magnetic field line.
 The radius of gyration (or gyroradius) 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=v_{\bot}/\Omega$
\end_inset

 is defined as the ratio of the magnitude of the particle's perpendicular
 velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{\bot}=|\mathbf{v}_{\bot}|$
\end_inset

 and the gyrofrequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=qB/mc$
\end_inset

.
 When the gyroradius 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is much smaller than the magnetic nonuniformity length-scale 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\bot}$
\end_inset

, the gyromotion is nearly circular and the action coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $mv_{\bot}^{2}/2\Omega\equiv(mc/q)\,\mu$
\end_inset

 associated with gyromotion is an adiabatic invariant (here 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\equiv mv_{\bot}^{2}/2B$
\end_inset

 denotes the magnetic moment).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The slower bounce and drift motions of a guiding-center are described by
 the Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $H=p_{\|}^{2}/2m+\mu\, B$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}$
\end_inset

 denotes the guiding-center's parallel momentum and the term 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\, B$
\end_inset

 plays the role of a spatial potential in which the guiding-center moves.
 The guiding-center equations of motion are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\dot{\mathbf{X}} & = & \frac{p_{\|}}{m}\;\frac{{\bf B}^{*}}{B_{\|}^{*}}\;+\;\epsilon\;\frac{c\,\bhat}{qB_{\|}^{*}}\btimes\mu\;\nabla B,\label{eq:xdot_gc}\\
\dot{p}_{\|} & = & -\;\frac{{\bf B}^{*}}{B_{\|}^{*}}\bdot\mu\;\nabla B,\label{ppardot_gc}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 where the modified magnetic fields 
\begin_inset Formula ${\bf B}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\|}^{*}\equiv\bhat\bdot{\bf B}^{*}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
{\bf B}^{*}\;\equiv\;{\bf B}\;+\;\epsilon\;(p_{\|}c/q)\;\nabla\btimes\bhat\;=\; B_{\|}^{*}\;\bhat\;+\;\epsilon\;(p_{\|}c/q)\;\bhat\btimes\left(\bhat\bdot\nabla\bhat\right).\label{eq:Bstar}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The first term in the guiding-center velocity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xdot_gc"

\end_inset

) includes the parallel velocity and the curvature drift velocity, while
 the second term represents the grad-B drift velocity.
 Both first-order drift velocities are, of course, involved in the slow
 drift motion of a guiding-center across magnetic-field lines.
 In Eqs.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xdot_gc"

\end_inset

)-(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Bstar"

\end_inset

), the small ordering parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\equiv\rho/L_{B}$
\end_inset

 is defined in terms of the ratio of the gyroradius 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 and the magnetic nonuniformity length scale 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{B}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bounce (or transit) motion of the guiding-center along a magnetic-field
 line is determined by the parallel spatial nonuniformity along the magnetic-fie
ld line.
 Indeed, to lowest order in magnetic-field nonuniformity, the guiding-center
 parallel-force equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "ppardot_gc"

\end_inset

) is expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{p}_{\|}=-\,\mu\,\partial B/\partial s$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 denotes the parallel spatial coordinate evaluated along a single magnetic-field
 line (with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat\equiv\partial\mathbf{X}/\partial s$
\end_inset

).
 To lowest order in 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

, we also have 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}\equiv m\,\dot{s}+{\cal O}(\epsilon)$
\end_inset

.
 By conservation of energy 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal E}=p^{2}/2m$
\end_inset

 and magnetic moment 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

, a guiding-center may encounter a turning point along a field line where
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}$
\end_inset

 vanishes (at a bounce point 
\begin_inset Formula $s=s_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{s}\equiv0$
\end_inset

) and a guiding-center may become 
\shape italic
trapped
\shape default
 between two such turning points.
 Otherwise, a 
\shape italic
passing
\shape default
 guiding-center continues its parallel transit motion along a field line
 without reversing its direction (i.e., the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}$
\end_inset

 becomes an invariant).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of the present work is to study the guiding-center Hamiltonian
 dynamics of a deeply-trapped charged particle.
\end_layout

