\begin_layout Section
Axisymmetric Dipole geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin with the expression for a pure-dipole magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}\;=\;\frac{{\cal M}}{r^{3}}\;\left(2\cos\theta\;\wh r\;+\;\sin\theta\;\wh{\theta}\right),\label{eq:Bdip_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where spherical coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(r,\theta,\phi)$
\end_inset

 are used and the constant 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal M}\equiv B_{\mathrm{e}}\, r_{\mathrm{e}}^{3}$
\end_inset

 combines the strength of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 at the equatorial radial distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 (at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset

).
 We can also write the magnetic field (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Bdip_def"

\end_inset

) in flux-coordinate representation 
\begin_inset Formula ${\bf B}=\nabla\psi\btimes\nabla\phi$
\end_inset

, where the poloidal flux 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\psi(r,\theta)\;=\;\frac{{\cal M}}{r}\;\sin^{2}\theta.\label{eq:psi_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Since a magnetic field line lies on a constant-
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 surface (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula ${\bf B}\bdot\nabla\psi=0$
\end_inset

), we find that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
r(\theta)\;=\; r_{\mathrm{e}}\;\sin^{2}\theta\label{eq:r_theta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 on a single magnetic field line (with 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal M}\equiv\psi_{\mathrm{e}}\, r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

), where the equatorial radial distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 (at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset

) can be used as a field label.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r_theta"

\end_inset

) allows us to introduce the distance along a magnetic field line (labeled
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
s(r_{\mathrm{e}},\theta)\;\equiv\; r_{\mathrm{e}}\;\int_{\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\theta}\;\left(1+3\cos^{2}\theta^{\prime}\right)^{1/2}\;\sin\theta^{\prime}\; d\theta^{\prime},\label{eq:s_def}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which is defined to be zero positive for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta>\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (below the equator), negative for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (above the equator), and zero at the equator 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta=\pi/2)$
\end_inset

.
 The integral in Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:s_def"

\end_inset

) can be evaluated explicitly, and we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
s(r_{\mathrm{e}},\theta)\;=\;\frac{r_{\mathrm{e}}}{2\sqrt{3}}\;\left[\;\ln\left(\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta}\;-\;\sqrt{3}\cos\theta\right)\;-\;\sqrt{3}\cos\theta\;\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\theta}\;\right].\label{eq:s_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The magnetic-field strength on a single line, on the other hand, is given
 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
B(\theta)\;\equiv\; B_{\mathrm{e}}\;(1+3\,\cos^{2}\theta)^{1/2}/\sin^{6}\theta,\label{eq:B_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which clearly shows that, in this pure-dipole model, all particles are
 trapped (since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 becomes infinite as 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

).
 We note that, by combining Eqs.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:s_dipole"

\end_inset

)-(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:B_dipole"

\end_inset

), the magnitude of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $B(s)$
\end_inset

 can be represented parametrically as a function of the parallel spatial
 coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Guiding-center Motion in Axisymmetric Dipole Geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the magnetic coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(\psi,s,\phi)$
\end_inset

 are used, the spatial Jacobian is 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal J}=(\nabla\psi\btimes\nabla s\bdot\nabla\phi)^{-1}=B^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 The parallel unit vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat$
\end_inset

 is expressed in terms of the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(\psi,s,\phi)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bhat\;=\;\nabla s\;+\; a(\psi,s)\;\nabla\psi,\label{eq:bhat_psis}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $a(\psi,s)\equiv\bhat\bdot\partial\mathbf{X}/\partial\psi$
\end_inset

.
 The curl of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat$
\end_inset

 is, therefore, expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla\btimes\bhat\;=\;\pd as\;\nabla s\btimes\nabla\psi\;\equiv\;\pd as\;\bhat\btimes\nabla\psi,\label{eq:curl_bhat_psis}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the magnetic curvature of a dipole field is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bhat\bdot\nabla\bhat\;=\;\pd as\;\nabla\psi.\label{eq:curv_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The magnetic-field gradient can be expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla B(\psi,s)\;=\;\pd B{\psi}\;\nabla\psi\;+\;\pd Bs\;\nabla s\;\equiv\;\left(\pd B{\psi}\;-\; a\;\pd Bs\right)\nabla\psi\;+\;\pd Bs\;\bhat,\label{eq:gradB_dipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 in terms its perpendicular components 
\begin_inset Formula $(\partial B/\partial\psi-a\;\partial B/\partial s)\,\nabla\psi$
\end_inset

 and its parallel component 
\begin_inset Formula $(\partial B/\partial s)\,\bhat$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bhat\btimes\nabla\psi$
\end_inset

 is directed in the toroidal direction only.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, the modified magnetic field (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Bstar"

\end_inset

) is expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
{\bf B}^{*}\;=\;\left(\nabla\phi\;+\;\rho_{\|}\,\pd as\;{\bf B}\right)\btimes\nabla\psi,\label{eq:Bstar_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\|}^{*}\equiv B$
\end_inset

, and the guiding-center velocity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xdot_gc"

\end_inset

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{\mathbf{x}}\;=\;\frac{v_{\|}}{B}\left(\nabla\phi\;+\;\rho_{\|}\,\pd as\;{\bf B}\right)\btimes\nabla\psi\;-\;\frac{c\mu}{qB}\;(\nabla\psi\bdot\nabla\ln B)\;\nabla\phi,\label{eq:xdotgc_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\nabla\psi\bdot\nabla\ln B\;=\;|\nabla\psi|^{2}\left(\pd{\ln B}{\psi}\;-\; a\;\pd{\ln B}s\right).\]

\end_inset

 The components of the guiding-center velocity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xdotgc_dip"

\end_inset

) are therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\dot{\psi}\\
\dot{s}\\
\dot{\phi}\end{array}\right)\;=\;\dot{\mathbf{x}}\bdot\nabla\left(\begin{array}{c}
\psi\\
s\\
\phi\end{array}\right)\;=\;\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
v_{\|}\\
\omega_{\mathrm{d}}\end{array}\right),\label{eq:psisphi_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where the guiding-center toroidal drift frequency is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\omega_{\mathrm{d}}\;\equiv\;\frac{c}{q}\left[\mu\left(\pd B{\psi}\;-\; a\;\pd Bs\right)\;+\; m\, v_{\|}^{2}\;\pd as\right].\label{eq:omega_phi}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 When the energy coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal E}$
\end_inset

 is used instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\|}$
\end_inset

, the drift frequency (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:omega_phi"

\end_inset

) may be rewritten as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{\phi}\;=\;\frac{c\mu}{q}\;\pd B{\psi}\;+\; v_{\|}\;\pd{}s\left(a\;\frac{cp_{\|}}{q}\right).\label{eq:phi_dot}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The guiding-center parallel acceleration, on the other hand, is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot{v}_{\|}\;=\;-\;\frac{\mu}{m}\;{\bf B}^{*}\bdot\nabla\ln B\;=\;-\;\frac{\mu}{m}\;\pd Bs.\label{eq:vdot_dip}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lastly, the invariance of the magnetic flux allows us to introduce the drift
 action 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{\mathrm{d}}\equiv-\, e\,\psi/c$
\end_inset

, which is canonically conjugate to the toroidal angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 in the guiding-center phase-space Lagrangian 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left(\frac{q}{c}\,{\bf A}+mv_{\|}\,\bhat\right)\bdot\exd\mathbf{x}\;=\;-\;\frac{q}{c}\,\psi\;\nabla\phi\bdot\exd\mathbf{x}\;+\; mv_{\|}\;\left(\nabla s+a\,\nabla\psi\right)\bdot\exd\mathbf{x}\;=\;-\;\frac{q}{c}\,\psi\;\exd\phi\;+\; mv_{\|}\left(\exd s+a\;\exd\psi\right).\label{eq:drift_action}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Bounce action-angle coordinates for deeply-trapped particles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the present Section, we will investigate trapped-particle guiding-center
 motion in the vicinity of the equator 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta\simeq\pi/2)$
\end_inset

.
 For this purpose, we introduce the 
\shape italic
latitude
\shape default
 angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\equiv\theta-\pi/2$
\end_inset

 (with 
\begin_inset Formula $|\sigma|\ll\pi/2$
\end_inset

), so that the distance along a field line (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:s_dipole"

\end_inset

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
s & = & \frac{r_{\mathrm{e}}}{2\sqrt{3}}\;\left[\;\ln\left(\sqrt{1+3\sin^{2}\sigma}\;+\;\sqrt{3}\sin\sigma\right)\;+\;\sqrt{3}\sin\sigma\;\sqrt{1+3\sin^{2}\sigma}\;\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & r_{\mathrm{e}}\;\left(\sin\sigma\;+\;\frac{1}{2}\,\sin^{3}\sigma\;+\;\cdots\right)\;=\; r_{\mathrm{e}}\;\left(\sigma\;+\;\frac{1}{3}\,\sigma^{3}\;+\;\cdots\right),\label{eq:s_sigma}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 i.e., in the vicinity of the equator, 
\begin_inset Formula $|\sigma|=|s|/r_{\mathrm{e}}\ll1$
\end_inset

 represents a normalized parallel distance.
 In this approximation, the magnetic-field strength (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:B_dipole"

\end_inset

) yields the expression 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{B(\theta)}{B_{\mathrm{e}}}\;=\;\frac{(1+3\sin^{2}\sigma)^{1/2}}{\cos^{6}\sigma}\;=\;1\;+\;\frac{9}{2}\;\sin^{2}\sigma\;+\;\frac{75}{8}\;\sin^{4}\sigma\;+\;\cdots\;=\;1\;+\;\frac{9}{2}\;\sigma^{2}\;+\;\frac{63}{8}\;\sigma^{4}\;+\;\cdots.\label{eq:B_sigma}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Hence, in the vicinity of the equator, the guiding-center moves in the
 potential 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}\,(9\,\mu B_{\mathrm{e}})\;\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 of a harmonic oscillator with energy 
\begin_inset Formula ${\cal E}-\mu\, B_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A deeply-trapped particle orbit is characterized by a pitch-angle coordinate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi\equiv p_{\|}/p$
\end_inset

 whose magnitude is much smaller than unity.
 If we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

 as the equatorial pitch-angle coordinate, then conservation of magnetic
 moment yields the relation 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{1-\xi^{2}(\theta)}{B(\theta)}\;=\;\frac{1-\xi_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}{B_{\mathrm{e}}}.\label{eq:xi_theta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Hence, for a fixed value 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{\mathrm{e}}$
\end_inset

, the turning-point angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

 is a solution of the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\theta_{\mathrm{b}})/B_{\mathrm{e}}\equiv1/(1-\xi_{\mathrm{e}}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 For deeply-trapped particles with turning points in the vicinity of the
 equator (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|\ll1$
\end_inset

), we find 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{\mathrm{b}}^{\pm}\equiv\pi/2\pm\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

, and the equatorial pitch-angle coordinate is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|\;\equiv\;\frac{3}{\sqrt{2}}\;\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}\left(1\;-\;\frac{11}{8}\;\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}^{2}\;+\;\cdots\right).\label{eq:sigma_b}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:eq_pitch"

\end_inset

 shows the equatorial pitch-angle 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

, where we clearly see that, for deeply-trapped particles 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}\ll\pi/2)$
\end_inset

, the equatorial pitch-angle 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi_{\mathrm{e}}|$
\end_inset

 depends linearly on 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{b}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

